'''Suzuka Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|鈴鹿県}}, ''Suzuka-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located at the southern tip of the island of [[Ishijima]], where the [[Katsuran Sea]] meets the open [[Shendan Ocean]]. Its population as of 2026 was 1,211,458, with approximately 18% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Tajima, Suzuka|Tajima]] (pop. 214,505). Suzuka is the largest prefecture on Ishijima by area (6,457.08 km²) and one of the most sparsely populated, a reflection of its mountainous interior and the limited coastal plain available for settlement.

Suzuka is defined by two forces: the {{nihongo|'''Suzuka Uzushio'''|鈴鹿渦潮}}, one of Adonia's strongest tidal currents, which surges through the narrow strait between Ishijima and [[Yagikoshu]] with each change of the tide; and the [[Suzuka Rolling Stock Works]], the largest railway manufacturing facility in Okaiken, which produces the trains that connect the nation's islands and cities. The prefecture's position at the gateway between the Katsuran Sea and the Shendan Ocean has made it a strategic maritime and industrial crossroads, and the Uzushio's enormous energy—both destructive and productive—has shaped every aspect of Suzuka's history, economy, and culture.

The prefecture also includes the [[Awashima]] ({{lang|ja|粟島}}), a small island in the Katsuran Sea approximately 120 km west of the Ishijima mainland, which is the westernmost point of the prefecture and the site of the [[Awashima Lighthouse]], one of the most important navigational aids in the Katsuran Sea.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The southern tip of Ishijima was one of the last areas of the island to be permanently settled, due to the extreme tidal currents of the Suzuka Strait and the limited coastal plain. The [[Atsu]] communities who eventually established themselves along the Suzuka coast developed a distinctive maritime culture adapted to the Uzushio, using the tidal currents for navigation and harvesting the nutrient-rich waters for fish and shellfish.

The [[Kajika]] wayfinding expansion reached the Suzuka Strait relatively late, and the narrow, turbulent passage between Ishijima and Yagikoshu was considered one of the most dangerous waterways in the Shendan. The Kajika navigators developed the {{nihongo|'''Uzushio reading'''|渦潮読み|Uzushio-yomi}}, a technique for predicting the timing and intensity of the tidal currents by observing the patterns of the whirlpools, which remains a living tradition among the Suzuka fishermen.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Suzuka Domain ===
The [[Suzuka Domain]] ({{lang|ja|鈴鹿藩}}, ''Suzuka-han'') was established under the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a retainer territory of the [[Urayasu clan]], hereditary vassals of the Honda who controlled the southern maritime approaches to the Katsuran Sea. The domain's strategic position at the gateway between the Katsuran Sea and the Shendan Ocean made it one of the most important naval territories in the shogunate, and the [[Urayasu Naval Watch]], a system of coastal fortifications and signal fires that guarded the strait, was maintained throughout the shogunate period.

The domain's wealth was built on the Uzushio fishery. The tidal currents brought enormous quantities of nutrient-rich water through the strait, supporting a fishery that was among the most productive in Okaiken. The [[Suzuka dried fish]] ({{lang|ja|鈴鹿干物}}, ''Suzuka-himono''), made by drying the catch in the strong coastal winds, was a major trade commodity and the origin of the prefecture's dried fish tradition that continues to this day.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Suzuka Domain into Suzuka Prefecture. The new government immediately recognised the strategic value of the Suzuka Strait for national defence and maritime commerce, and the [[Suzuka Strait Lighthouse]] (later replaced by the [[Awashima Lighthouse]]) was one of the first public works projects of the Restoration era.

The 20th century saw two transformative developments. The first was the establishment of the [[Suzuka Rolling Stock Works]] in 1912, which grew from a small railway repair shop into the largest railway manufacturing facility in Okaiken. The second was the harnessing of the Uzushio's tidal energy: the [[Suzuka Tidal Power Station]], commissioned in 2008, was the first commercial-scale tidal power plant in Fosia and demonstrated the enormous potential of the strait's currents for clean energy generation.

== Geography ==

Suzuka Prefecture occupies the southern tip of Ishijima, stretching from the [[Katsuran Sea]] coast in the west to the [[Shendan Ocean]] coast in the east, with the [[Suzuka Strait]]—the narrow passage between Ishijima and [[Yagikoshu]]—forming the southern boundary. The prefecture is the largest on Ishijima by area (6,457.08 km²) but one of the most sparsely populated, with the majority of its population concentrated in the narrow coastal plains and the interior river valleys.

The {{nihongo|'''Suzuka Mountains'''|鈴鹿山脈|Suzuka-sanmyaku}} form the spine of the prefecture, rising to 1,200–1,800 m in the interior and creating a dramatic landscape of steep forested slopes, deep river gorges, and narrow coastal terraces. The mountains descend sharply to both the Katsuran Sea and the Shendan Ocean, leaving only narrow strips of coastal plain for settlement and industry.

The [[Suzuka Strait]] ({{lang|ja|鈴鹿海峡}}, ''Suzuka-kaikyō''), the narrow passage between Ishijima and Yagikoshu, is the prefecture's defining geographic feature. The strait is approximately 4.2 km wide at its narrowest point, and the funnel shape of the Katsuran Sea entrance forces the tidal flow through a constriction that accelerates the current to speeds of up to 20 km/h—making the {{nihongo|'''Suzuka Uzushio'''|鈴鹿渦潮}} one of the strongest tidal currents in Adonia. The Uzushio generates massive whirlpools that can be up to 30 metres in diameter during the spring tides, and the roar of the current can be heard from several kilometres away.

The [[Awashima]] ({{lang|ja|粟島}}), a small island approximately 120 km west of the Ishijima mainland in the Katsuran Sea, is the westernmost point of the prefecture. The island has a population of 210 and is the site of the [[Awashima Lighthouse]], a critical navigational aid for vessels entering the Katsuran Sea from the Shendan Ocean.

